{
	"version": 5,
	"rows": [
		{
			"id": "ewhsFmbBOcMU994Xs",
			"description": "Filter Mask",
			"local_notes": "A light filter mask covering nose and mouth; grants +2 to HT rolls to resist inhaled toxins, smoke and dust, but is not a full atmospheric seal and supplies no oxygen.\n\nLore: Basic breathing protection issued wherever the Munitorum expects toxic atmospheres, ash storms or gas warfare short of a full hazmat environment — seen throughout Astra Militarum background art and Black Library fiction set on hive or forge worlds.\n\nDesign: No armour/damage anchor applies; built as a simple GURPS filter mask (+2 to HT rolls vs. inhaled hazards) at a TL appropriate to \"stub tech\"-era mass production, since a filter mask is mechanically simple and produced across the Imperium regardless of a world's exact tech base. Named a filter mask rather than a rebreather because it has no oxygen supply: GURPS rebreathers are closed-circuit oxygen devices, and the Respirator below is the sealed set.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "8",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "60",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e6bZUAqohbYaoG3Nj",
			"description": "Respirator",
			"local_notes": "A full sealed mask with filter canisters, worn as a hood or integrated into a helmet; grants full protection from inhaled toxins and gas (not just a resistance bonus) while canisters last, and is the standard companion piece to the Death Korps Flak Coat.\n\nLore: Full-face gas-sealed respirators, most iconically the Death Korps of Krieg's ubiquitous gas-hoods, are depicted in Forge World's Imperial Armour background and Krieg-focused Black Library fiction as standard kit for regiments that expect chemical, biological or simply toxic battlefield conditions as a matter of course.\n\nDesign: Sits a clear step above the Filter Mask: full immunity rather than a resistance bonus, priced and weighted higher to reflect the canister filtration system, at the same \"standard Imperial manufacture\" TL as flak and carapace per framework.md's TL table.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "220",
			"base_weight": "3 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eh5J-v3xoW0lDg7Jp",
			"description": "Photo-Visor / Photo-Contacts",
			"local_notes": "Low-light image intensification, worn as a visor/goggle unit or, in the surgically-fitted \"photo-contacts\" variant, as lenses with no external bulk (same effect, -1 Holdout only for the visor version, none for contacts). Grants Night Vision 5 (no penalty for darkness up to -5) while worn/active; does not help in absolute darkness.\n\nLore: Photo-visors (and their subtler photo-contact lens equivalent) are common-issue night-fighting gear across Astra Militarum and Inquisitorial-agent background, letting the wearer fight effectively in low light without a powered searchlight giving away their position.\n\nDesign: No anchor; modelled as GURPS Night Vision 5 (Basic Set), a level between cheap goggles and the best military optics, noted in the rules text because equipment in this schema has no \"grant an advantage\" hook. Priced as inexpensive, widely issued optics rather than a rare relic.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "150",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e_DAHhjQFqvQqe4gS",
			"description": "Magnoculars",
			"local_notes": "Long-range magnifying optics with image stabilisation and, on most patterns, a basic infrared overlay for spotting heat sources at range.\n\nLore: Standard-issue field optics for officers, snipers and scouts across Astra Militarum background, functionally binoculars with a targeting and threat-marking overlay fed to a user's other gear (vox, auspex) where available.\n\nDesign: No anchor; treated as GURPS binoculars (Basic Set) with an infrared spotting note added for setting flavour, at Imperial-standard TL10 manufacture.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "300",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eIsYSeZNLhRnnL9wV",
			"description": "Auspex",
			"local_notes": "Handheld multi-sensor unit reading motion, heat, atmosphere and (on some patterns) psychic disturbance; the bonus above applies specifically to actively scanning an area, not passive Perception.\n\nLore: The auspex is the Imperium's ubiquitous field scanner, seen in nearly every piece of Inquisitorial, Astra Militarum and Adeptus Mechanicus fiction and background as the standard tool for detecting hidden enemies, structural hazards, or (in Inquisitorial hands) warp taint.\n\nDesign: No damage/armour anchor applies; modelled as a flat +3 to Electronics Operation (Sensors), the skill scanner use falls under (Search is a Per/vision skill and would not model it), following Basic Set's \"instruments help\" convention rather than lifted from any Ultra-Tech scanner entry, priced as valuable, restricted military-grade kit (LC2) consistent with its usefulness and relative scarcity outside the Guard and Inquisition.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "900",
			"base_weight": "4 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "Electronics Operation"
					},
					"amount": 3,
					"specialization": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "Sensors"
					}
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eZoCqt4RE7UW5ir3C",
			"description": "Vox-Caster",
			"local_notes": "Backpack-mounted long-range radio, typically crewed by a dedicated vox-operator; range about 5 miles over open ground (micro-bead about 1 mile).\n\nLore: The vox-caster is the standard heavy communications set of Astra Militarum platoons and companies, a recurring detail in Guard-focused Black Library fiction (a vox-operator is a near-universal squad role) and Codex Astra Militarum background.\n\nDesign: No anchor; built as a straightforward GURPS long-range radio (Basic Set), weighted and priced to reflect a dedicated backpack set rather than a personal comm-bead.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "400",
			"base_weight": "12 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eYNILM6pHAA4qz7lw",
			"description": "Micro-bead",
			"local_notes": "Short-range personal earpiece/throat-mic comm unit for squad-level chatter; range about 1 mile over open ground, a fifth of a vox-caster's.\n\nLore: The micro-bead is standard personal-issue comms for individual Guardsmen and many other Imperial servants, referenced constantly in Black Library fiction as the earpiece through which squad orders and chatter travel.\n\nDesign: Short-range GURPS radio (Basic Set) at minimal weight and cost, deliberately much cheaper and shorter-ranged than the Vox-Caster to reflect the personal-vs-squad-support distinction the lore consistently draws between the two.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "80",
			"base_weight": "0.25 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "el2izu1rAyKYH-LQS",
			"description": "Preysense Goggles",
			"local_notes": "Combined thermal/motion overlay goggles, worn under a gas-hood visor; grants Night Vision 5 as a photo-visor, plus motion-detection against concealed or camouflaged targets at the GM's discretion.\n\nLore: Preysense goggles are a signature piece of Death Korps of Krieg kit in Forge World's Imperial Armour background, combining thermal imaging with motion sensing to let Krieg troopers fight effectively through the toxic smoke and fog typical of their siege warfare.\n\nDesign: Built as an upgraded Photo-Visor (adds motion detection on top of low-light vision) rather than copied from any catalogue scanner; priced and weighted similarly to the Photo-Visor with a modest premium for the added sensor mode.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "350",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eg_i-bGcc0igYOJ3Y",
			"description": "Grapnel Launcher",
			"local_notes": "Compressed-gas or micro-charge grapnel and line, used for scaling walls, boarding actions and rapid descent; treat as a GURPS grapnel-gun/pneumatic harpoon under Climbing rules.\n\nLore: Grapnel lines and launchers appear throughout Imperial special-operations depictions — Scions and other elite Guard, Sisters of Battle strike teams and Inquisitorial agents alike are shown using them for boarding actions and rapid vertical assault.\n\nDesign: No anchor; modelled as a standard GURPS grapnel tool (Basic Set climbing gear) rather than any catalogue \"descent device,\" priced and weighted for a compact personal-issue unit.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "250",
			"base_weight": "5 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "efG4QpvTdmi63DYl9",
			"description": "Jump Pack (Astartes)",
			"local_notes": "Rocket-propelled hop pack: Flight for short powered jumps, not sustained flight. Air Move 15, 6 seconds of thrust per fuel load (each turn of thrust uses 1 second), and it cannot hover: the wearer climbs on burn turns and must be descending or landing when the fuel is gone. A burning jump can carry a wearer about 60 yards up and 100 yards across. Refuel takes about 10 minutes and a Ready maneuver to prime. Anyone within 2 yards of a landing takes 1d burn from the exhaust wash. Sized and rigged for the Astartes frame and its power-armour interface: the 60 lb frame and thrust are more than an unaugmented human can control in Astartes plate (the Sororitas version below is the human-scaled pack).\n\nLore: Jump packs equip Assault Marines and Vanguard veterans across Codex Space Marines background and Black Library fiction, covering the ground between a standard Astartes's leap and true flight in short, thunderous bursts, at the cost of a very conspicuous, fuel-limited profile.\n\nDesign: Built as a limited Flight capability in the style of the Ultra-Tech jet pack (Air Move on burn turns, no hover, short fuel) with numbers set here: Air Move 15 (30 yards over two turns) and 6 seconds of fuel give the 60-yard, 100-yard hops the fiction shows, without making Assault Marines into flyers. Schema note: equipment has no Flight hook, so the capability sits in the rules text. Weight and cost scaled to sit well above mundane Guard equipment, in line with other Astartes-exclusive wargear in this file.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "45000",
			"base_weight": "60 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "ewCN4PsNzbjCWQSun",
			"description": "Jump Pack (Sororitas Seraphim)",
			"local_notes": "As the Astartes jump pack but sized for an unaugmented human in Sororitas plate: Air Move 10, 4 seconds of thrust per fuel load, no hover, about 35 yards up and 50 yards across. Exhaust wash as the Astartes pack (1d burn within 2 yards of a landing). Refuel about 10 minutes.\n\nLore: Seraphim squads fly with jump packs and are drawn as the Sisters' fast, hard-hitting assault troops in Codex Adepta Sororitas background. They are unaugmented humans, so their packs are lighter and weaker than the Astartes pattern.\n\nDesign: The Astartes jump pack scaled down to a human frame: half the weight, two thirds of the Air Move and fuel, priced at 40% of the Astartes pack because it is smaller but also an issue item of a large organisation. LC1 rather than LC0, matching Sororitas power weapons.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adepta Sororitas"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "1",
			"base_value": "18000",
			"base_weight": "30 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e9gR3Jq0Hb_MMDtv7",
			"description": "Grav-chute",
			"local_notes": "Anti-grav-assisted parachute harness used for high-altitude insertion; converts what would otherwise be a lethal fall into a controlled descent.\n\nLore: Grav-chutes equip Guard airborne/drop formations (Elysian and similar regiments in Codex and Black Library background) for high-altitude, low-opening insertions from Valkyrie and Aquila lander drops.\n\nDesign: No anchor; modelled as a GURPS parachute/descent device (Basic Set) with a \"grav-assisted\" label for setting flavour, priced and weighted as specialist rather than universal Guard kit, hence the more restrictive LC2.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "500",
			"base_weight": "8 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eQTDWnryCQYPawV7a",
			"description": "Medi-kit",
			"local_notes": "Standard combat first-aid kit; the bonus above applies to First Aid rolls made using it, per its contents (counter-septic, sealant foam, stimm shots, basic surgical tools).\n\nLore: Combat medi-kits are standard issue to Guard medicae and squad \"doc\" characters throughout Astra Militarum background and fiction, from field dressings to combat stimms.\n\nDesign: Direct translation of GURPS's standard first-aid kit bonus (Basic Set) rather than a Chirurgeon's full surgery kit, since this is field-carried personal gear rather than a hospital set.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "180",
			"base_weight": "4 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "First Aid"
					},
					"amount": 2
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "E6VPO9G5sRLeAz2qf",
			"description": "Guardsman's Kit",
			"local_notes": "The standard web-gear/pack loadout carried by an Astra Militarum trooper on campaign.\n\nLore: The contents below are drawn from how Guard kit is consistently depicted across Black Library fiction (the Gaunt's Ghosts and Ciaphas Cain series in particular) and Astra Militarum background: unglamorous, faith-adjacent, and built around long marches and worse rations.\n\nDesign: A container grouping the small items below into one carried loadout, rather than tracking each separately. GCS adds a container's children to its own weight and cost, so the container itself is 0 lb and $0 and the total is the sum of the parts (22.5 lb, $90).",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "4",
			"base_value": "0",
			"base_weight": "0 lb",
			"quantity": 1,
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "eIoy64wvkg0n7CN3Z",
					"description": "Uplifting Primer",
					"local_notes": "The Munitorum-issue catechism and basic-literacy primer given to every Guardsman; also a minor icon of faith.\n\nLore: A recurring, near-universal detail of Guard life in Black Library fiction — often quoted, occasionally mocked by veterans, carried by nearly everyone regardless.\n\nDesign: Flavour item; no mechanical effect.",
					"base_value": "5",
					"base_weight": "1 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "e3skWTgV-3QrMCNXQ",
					"description": "Lho-sticks (pack)",
					"local_notes": "A pack of rolled lho-leaf cigarettes, the Guard's ubiquitous off-duty vice.\n\nLore: Referenced constantly in Guard-focused fiction as shared currency, a nerve-settler before battle, and a habit officers alternately tolerate and ban.\n\nDesign: Flavour item; no mechanical effect.",
					"base_value": "10",
					"base_weight": "0.25 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "eOf86nRhdQrs6tA8g",
					"description": "Ration Packs (week's supply)",
					"local_notes": "Preserved, nutritionally complete but famously unpleasant field rations.\n\nLore: A running joke and occasional plot point across Guard fiction, where rations are barely tolerable and traded for anything better.\n\nDesign: Standard GURPS travel rations (Basic Set) by another name, priced accordingly.",
					"base_value": "15",
					"base_weight": "10 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "ej9DXcV1WorowSVot",
					"description": "Bedroll",
					"local_notes": "Standard-issue sleeping roll.\n\nLore: Unremarkable but universal Guard kit.\n\nDesign: Flavour item; no mechanical effect.",
					"base_value": "15",
					"base_weight": "4 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "eoslSb65lr13OB3gk",
					"description": "Mess Kit & Canteen",
					"local_notes": "Collapsible mess tin, utensils and a water canteen.\n\nLore: Standard Guard kit, occasionally a plot detail when water runs short.\n\nDesign: Flavour item; no mechanical effect.",
					"base_value": "10",
					"base_weight": "2 lb",
					"quantity": 1,
					"equipped": true
				},
				{
					"id": "eeM6eRmDSHaEexjdw",
					"description": "Entrenching Tool, Folding",
					"local_notes": "Doubles as a digging tool; treat as an improvised weapon (already priced and statted as one, no further penalty needed when it's used as intended). The U means it cannot parry in a turn in which it has attacked, nor attack in a turn in which it has parried (B269) - a heavy, head-forward tool is slow to bring back on guard.\n\nLore: Standard Guard field-engineering kit, folded and slung on webbing; depicted in trench-warfare-flavoured Guard fiction being swung in desperate close combat as often as it's used to dig in, in the tradition of real-world entrenching-tool improvised weapons.\n\nDesign: Not anchored; built as a short, heavy-headed improvised axe/mace per Basic Set logic - a folding steel spade has enough mass to crush but a poor edge and balance, giving sw+1 cr, reach 1, min ST 8. TL5 reflects the folding steel tool itself (an industrial product), not the Imperium's overall manufacturing TL. Handling: parry corrected to 0U, the Basic Set convention for a head-heavy chopping/crushing tool (Axe/Mace family, B271): it is worse to parry with than a purpose-built one-handed weapon, and swinging a lopsided spade-head leaves it out of position to parry again right away. Weight (5 lb) and min ST (8) are unchanged.",
					"tags": [
						"Melee Weapon",
						"Imperium",
						"Astra Militarum"
					],
					"tech_level": "5",
					"legality_class": "4",
					"base_value": "30",
					"base_weight": "5 lb",
					"weapons": [
						{
							"id": "wbw0eGF00TlpbSHHx",
							"sv": 1,
							"damage": {
								"type": "cr",
								"st": "sw",
								"base": "1"
							},
							"reach": "1",
							"parry": "0U",
							"strength": "8",
							"usage": "Spade edge",
							"defaults": [
								{
									"type": "dx",
									"modifier": -5
								},
								{
									"type": "skill",
									"name": {
										"compare": "is",
										"qualifier": "Axe/Mace"
									}
								},
								{
